# the x86 field kinds: legacy prefixes, rex, rex2, vex, evex and xop, the opcode, ModRM, SIB and displacement, absolute
# addresses and string memory
#
# an x86 instruction is a run of legacy prefixes, a rex or rex2 prefix in
# 64-bit code or a vex, evex or xop prefix, the opcode's escape bytes and its
# opcode byte, a ModRM byte, SIB byte and displacement, and its immediates,
# 3dnow's opcode the byte after them.
# every field of a row reads one opcode record (Opcode), which the generator
# states once per row and the row's fields point at: its encoding space, map,
# opcode byte, mandatory prefix, operand size, vector length, and the operands
# in ModRM.reg and rm, in the opcode's low bits, in vvvv, the opmask, `{z}`
# and the rounding, and the memory operand the prefixes apply to. the kinds
# are:
#   head    the legacy prefixes, rex, rex2, vex, evex or xop, the escape bytes and the opcode
#   modrm   ModRM, SIB and the displacement of a register or memory operand, a vector index (VSIB) or an opcode
#           extension in ModRM.rm, an evex disp8 scaled by its N
#   moffs   the absolute address of the a0 to a3 moves, as wide as the address size
#   string  a string instruction's memory, at the one register of the row's address size, which no byte encodes
# immediates, relative targets, vex's /is4 register and 3dnow's opcode are the core's units.
#
# what the prefixes mean is a function of the code width (the context's
# width state), the row, the operands' values and the form, which the kinds
# compute alike on encode and decode (needs, layout), so no fact passes
# between fields. on decode each kind reads the prefixes it needs from the
# instruction's start (scan), and a part of an operand is deposited by the
# one kind that encodes it: ModRM's operands by modrm, with their rex bits,
# and the opcode's register, vvvv's, the opmask, `{z}`, a broadcast and the
# rounding by head. head completes the decode once every field is read,
# writing the form bits of whatever the bytes chose that the zero form would
# not: a prefix no operand needs, a payload bit no operand decides, a prefix
# run that is not the canonical one, an ignored vector length, c4 where c5
# would do, the evex space where the vex twin would do, the reg-from-rm
# direction. the displacement's width, a needless SIB byte and an address size
# override no register states are the address's kind's.
#
# an instruction two spaces encode, as vaddps by vex and evex, is two rows,
# and the one that gives way states the reach of its twin (Opcode.twin): it
# refuses operands its twin holds unless the form names its space, so either
# row takes exactly what the other leaves and no choice hangs on the order
# rows are tried in.
#
# sources, read as published:
#   Intel SDM 325462-093US, Volume 2A, 2.1 to 2.2: the prefix groups, ModRM and SIB (Tables 2-1 to 2-3), rex
#   Intel SDM 325462-093US, Volume 2A, 2.3 and 2.7: vex, evex, embedded rounding, broadcast and compressed disp8
#   Intel SDM 325462-093US, Volume 2A, 2.3.12 and 2.7.9: VSIB, the vector index of a gather or scatter
#   AMD APM 24594 Vol 3, 1.8 and Appendix A.3: the xop prefix and 3dnow's 0f 0f, its opcode after ModRM
#   Intel APX 355828-009US, 3.1: the rex2 prefix, its payload and the maps it reaches, evex's extended registers, nd
#                                and nf
#   llvm-mc 23.1.1: the canonical prefix order (segment, 67, 66, f0, then f2 or f3) and every expected encoding below

